    "id": "matchup.bell_race",
    "name": "Jotaro and Kakyoin vs the Requiem Bell and the Iron Brawler",
    "description": "The Requiem Bell's home encounter and the fourth fixture built from the ambush beside a constant Iron Brawler. This one is a clock rather than an attrition test, and it is the only enemy in issue #16 that landed inside its band on the first reading: 68% against 48..80, with no tuning pass, where the Blade Dancer needed three readings and the Hex Weaver five. The Bell acts on tick 25 and, if it lives, again on tick 50, and the party's job is to arrive between those two moments. Measured, it takes 1.24 actions per battle and finishes with alive% 7%, so the race is real and the party wins it about three times in four; the second toll landed in roughly 24% of battles rather than the coin flip predicted. Two models were recorded before the run instead of one, and the reading settled them: counting all party damage per action at 95.6 put the Bell's death near tick 48, counting only what lowest-HP focus fire lands on it at about 64 put it near tick 52, and the first model won. The correction is specific and reusable - the 0.6 to 0.65 multiplier taken from FOCUS_FIRE_CHANCE 55 is too pessimistic when the low-HP foe STAYS the low-HP foe, because every re-selection finds the same target and the redirection compounds in one direction instead of averaging out. The band is 32 points wide on the argument that a fixture turning on whether one action happens cannot honestly carry a narrow one; at 68% against a centre of 64 that width was not needed, but it was argued from weaver_gambit's measured 1.45 points of win rate per 1% of party damage per action, so it stays as argued rather than being narrowed after the fact. Two findings here are about the harness rather than the content. First, median turns read 17, below assassin_ambush's 21 and dancer_rush's 22, against a predicted 20..24 - and that is NOT a shorter fight. turns counts ACTIONS, and a foe at action rate 0.0242 contributes almost none of them, so 11.47 party actions at a combined 0.169 still spans about 68 ticks, comparable to both siblings. Median turns stops being a duration once combatant speeds differ this much, and reading it as one is what produced the miss. Second, the Iron Brawler came in at 590, an eighth distinct value from identical stats across 531, 590, 621, 631, 656, 713, 720 and 763, which keeps saying that foe output is not a per-combatant constant. One honest limit on the design: 38% of battles never hear a toll at all, because the aggressive profile rolls AGGRESSIVE_SKILL_CHANCE 65 before it scores anything. An encounter built around a single signature action gets that action about two turns in three however the tempo is arranged. That is a property of the AI rather than of this content, and it is the ceiling on how reliably content of this shape can read.",
